All these deposits are on Borealis (https://borealisdata.ca).

The three 1998 surveys cover francophones only. The codebook of the deposit
says it was decided to keep only francophones ("retenir uniquement les
francophones"), because CREATEC had surveyed only the francophone
population. The CREATEC file holds respondents whose mother tongue is
French (question Q17: all 1,057); the CROP file interviewed 450
respondents in French and kept, for its post-election wave, the 426 whose
language spoken most often in the household is French (question q22). The
combined qes1998 panel file pools these 1,057 CREATEC and 426 CROP
respondents: its rows link one to one to the firms' files (quetr; for CROP,
quest = quetr - 60000), which confirms the definition firm by firm.


2. The 2022 study (CC BY-NC 4.0): no labels, question text or counts ship
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

  qes2022             2022 Quebec Election Study. Mahéo, Bélanger,        10.7910/DVN/PAQBDR
                      Stephenson, Harell (Harvard Dataverse)

This study is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0
(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/). Until its authors agree
in writing, qesR ships none of its value labels, question text or answer
counts.
The package holds only its catalog entry (title, authors, version, file
names, sizes and checksums, as published on the deposit), the names of the
variables whose weights and interview dates get_qes_master() reads
(inst/extdata/harmonize/legacy.csv), rules written for qesR that say which
codes mark item nonresponse (inst/extdata/dict/shard_rules.csv), and the
rows of the harmonization spec (inst/extdata/harmonize/): variable names,
codes, the md5 hash of each normalized value label (used to check that the
labels of your copy are the ones the spec was written for, without shipping
them), page references to its codebook, and the facts about its waves and
weights that the codebook publishes (pp. 5 and 7): the size of each wave,
its fieldwork dates and mode, and the weighting method (raking on age,
gender, education and language, the trimming bounds). The notes and grade
reasons of the spec rows say, in qesR's own words and with page references
to the codebook, how each question was asked and routed and which level or
missing-value reason each code is given (as the value maps do); they quote
no value label or question text. No count of answers from its data file
ships: gates.csv and expected/marginals.csv have no qes2022 row, and the
test fixture tests/testthat/fixtures/v044-get-qes-names.csv gives, for
qes2022, only the variable names and storage types that qesR 0.4.4
returned, with its column of missing-value counts left empty.
expected/hashes.csv holds, for each of its harmonized columns, the number
of rows of the file (1,521, the size of the study, which the codebook
publishes on p. 5) and the md5 hash of the column that qesR computes from
the pinned file (used to check that the harmonization gives the same
result on your copy); a hash cannot be turned back into answers or counts.
Its codebook
is built on your computer from your own copy of the data file. When you use
this study, the licence requires attribution (qes_cite("qes2022")) and rules
out commercial use.

The one exception is outside the package: the source repository of qesR
(not the package built from it) keeps, in the folder data-raw/nc/, counts
and other aggregates of the 2022 study and the value labels that mark its
item nonresponse, for the checks run in GitHub CI. That folder is excluded
from the package build. Its files are licensed under CC BY-NC 4.0, with the
attribution above, and are not covered by the MIT licence of qesR
(data-raw/nc/README.md).


3. Other sources
----------------

  - inst/extdata/catalog/elections.csv: election dates, from the official
    results published by Élections Québec (each row gives its source URL).
  - inst/extdata/harmonize/legacy.csv: how each column of get_qes_master()
    and get_decon() is rendered from the harmonized targets; and
    inst/extdata/legacy/: the columns qesR 0.4.4 stacked by name and no
    longer built (removed.csv), and the studies whose legacy values differ
    from qesR 0.4.4 (changes.csv, no counts); part of qesR (MIT).
  - Not in the package: inst/extdata/validation/official_results.csv and
    official_turnout.csv of the qesR source repository hold province-wide
    votes by party, valid and rejected ballots, ballots cast and registered
    electors of the Quebec general elections of 1998 to 2022, from the
    official results published by Élections Québec (its archived results
    files; each row gives its source URL). Parties other than those the
    harmonization spec names are summed into one row. Source: Élections
    Québec, official results of the general elections, © Directeur général
    des élections du Québec. Two sets of terms apply (consulted
    2026-09-27):
      (a) 2014, 2018 and 2022. Their results files are listed on dgeq.org,
      Élections Québec's open-data site, and are covered by its open-data
      licence, the "Licence d'utilisation des données ouvertes du
      directeur général des élections" (https://www.dgeq.org/licence.html;
      in English, https://www.dgeq.org/en/licence.html): free,
      non-exclusive and non-transferable, for any lawful purpose, commercial
      use included, with the rights to reproduce, translate, compile,
      communicate to the public and sub-license. It requires this notice at
      all times: « Comprend des données ouvertes octroyées sous la licence
      d'utilisation des données ouvertes du directeur général des
      élections disponible à l'adresse Web dgeq.org. L'octroi de la
      licence n'implique aucune approbation par le directeur général des
      élections de l'utilisation des données ouvertes qui en est faite. »
      ("Contains open data granted under the Chief Electoral Officer's Open
      Data User Licence available at http://dgeq.org/en/. The fact of
      granting the licence in no way implies that the Chief Electoral
      Officer approves the use made of the open data.") The user must also
